\section{Interim results}
\label{sec:results}

\noindent\textit{Status of fieldwork.} This section is regenerated from the most recent export (\Sexpr{data_date}). It uses \Sexpr{paper_counts$completed_respondents} eligible completed responses---\Sexpr{paper_counts$baseline_respondents} in \armB, \Sexpr{paper_counts$time_respondents} in \armT, and \Sexpr{paper_counts$cost_respondents} in \armC---or \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", paper_counts$percent_of_target)} percent of the planned \Sexpr{paper_counts$target_respondents}. They contribute \Sexpr{format(paper_counts$analytical_tasks, big.mark = ",")} analytical tasks and \Sexpr{format(paper_counts$candidate_appearances, big.mark = ",")} candidate appearances. These are interim monitoring estimates: they do not define a significance-based stopping rule, and the preregistered analysis of the complete sample will supersede them. Recruitment, sample composition, and implementation checks are in Appendix~\ref{app:collection}.

Table~\ref{tab:variables} describes every variable used in the analysis at the level at which it is measured, overall and by condition. Panel C doubles as a randomization check on the displayed profiles, and panel A shows how far the conditions differ by chance in the pretreatment covariates that enter Equation~\eqref{eq:lin}. Table~\ref{tab:primary} reports every preregistered primary test: the main preregistered difference, the complementary regression-adjusted estimate, and which of the two reject zero after Holm correction. Figures~\ref{fig:process}--\ref{fig:profiles} display the corresponding estimands. \Sexpr{live(evidence_summary())}. The ratio of regression-adjusted to preregistered standard errors has a median of \Sexpr{live(se_ratio_text(family_process))} in the process family and \Sexpr{live(se_ratio_text(family_gender))} in the gender-interaction family; ratios below one are precision gains from adjustment. These gains are bounded by the design: the process conditions vary only across respondents, so only respondent characteristics can absorb noise, and the preregistered gender estimator already compares women and men within respondents.

