\subsection{Information acquisition and decision speed}

Figure~\ref{fig:process} reports the primary process outcomes. Under \armB, respondents opened at least one description in \Sexpr{live(mean_value("Any information", "Baseline"))} percent of tasks and opened \Sexpr{live(mean_value("Descriptions revealed", "Baseline", 2))} of the two descriptions on average. Relative to \armB, the difference in the probability of opening any description ($\tau^A_{zB}$) was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row("Any information", "Time"), 100, 1, " percentage points"))} under \armT\ and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row("Any information", "Cost"), 100, 1, " percentage points"))} under \armC; the regression-adjusted estimates were \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Any information", "Time - Baseline"))} and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Any information", "Cost - Baseline"))}. The difference in the number of descriptions opened ($\tau^K_{zB}$) was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row("Descriptions revealed", "Time"), 1, 2, ""))} under \armT\ and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row("Descriptions revealed", "Cost"), 1, 2, ""))} under \armC, against regression-adjusted estimates of \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Descriptions revealed", "Time - Baseline"))} and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Descriptions revealed", "Cost - Baseline"))}. Reading the two estimators together, \Sexpr{live(agreement_text("Any information"))}, and \Sexpr{live(agreement_text("Descriptions revealed"))}.

The theory expects the two constraints to act differently on timing: a deadline should speed the first selection, whereas an access cost should slow it, because information must be earned before it is used. Relative to \armB, the difference in capped time to first selection ($\tau^{D^{9}}_{zB}$) was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row("Capped first-selection time (s)", "Time"), 1, 2, " seconds"))} under \armT\ and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row("Capped first-selection time (s)", "Cost"), 1, 2, " seconds"))} under \armC. The share of tasks without a selection within nine seconds ($\tau^{S^{9}}_{zB}$) changed by \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row(process_outcomes$outcome[4], "Time"), 100, 1, " percentage points"))} under \armT\ and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(process_row(process_outcomes$outcome[4], "Cost"), 100, 1, " percentage points"))} under \armC. The regression-adjusted estimates were \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Capped first-selection time (s)", "Time - Baseline", " seconds"))} and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Capped first-selection time (s)", "Cost - Baseline", " seconds"))} for $\tau^{D^{9}}_{zB}$, and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text(process_outcomes$outcome[4], "Time - Baseline", " percentage points"))} and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text(process_outcomes$outcome[4], "Cost - Baseline", " percentage points"))} for $\tau^{S^{9}}_{zB}$. After Holm correction, \Sexpr{live(agreement_text("Capped first-selection time (s)"))}, and \Sexpr{live(agreement_text(process_outcomes$outcome[4]))}. Figure~\ref{fig:process}B shows each contrast under both estimators.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-process, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=5.0, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Information acquisition and decision speed by decision environment}
\label{fig:process}
\begin{notes}
Panel A: observed condition means with respondent-clustered 95\% confidence intervals. Panel B: differences from \armB\ ($\tau_{TB}$, $\tau_{CB}$); filled circles are the main preregistered differences in means (Equation~\eqref{eq:dim}), hollow diamonds the complementary regression-adjusted estimates (Equation~\eqref{eq:lin}: centered age, gender, tertiary education, and political interest, interacted with condition). Proportions are in percent and their differences in percentage points; intervals for proportions are truncated at 0 and 100 for display. Time to first selection is capped at \Sexpr{selection_window_seconds} seconds in every condition, and the no-selection outcome uses the same window, so it is not a terminal timeout. \armT: \Sexpr{deadline_seconds}-second deadline. \armC: arithmetic problem required to open each description. \Sexpr{format(paper_counts$analytical_tasks, big.mark = ",")} analytical tasks from \Sexpr{paper_counts$completed_respondents} eligible completed responses.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Gendered scrutiny and selection}

Figure~\ref{fig:gender} turns to the candidate-level estimands. Under \armB, the women-minus-men difference in the probability of opening a candidate's description was $\Gamma_M(B)=$ \Sexpr{live(effect_text(gamma_row("Information acquisition", "Baseline"), 100, 1, " percentage points"))}, and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Information acquisition", "Baseline", " points"))} as a within-respondent difference from Equation~\eqref{eq:gamma}; H3 anticipates a positive gap. The change in this gap was $\Psi_M(T)=$ \Sexpr{live(effect_text(psi_row("Information acquisition", "Time"), 100, 1, " points"))} under \armT\ and $\Psi_M(C)=$ \Sexpr{live(effect_text(psi_row("Information acquisition", "Cost"), 100, 1, " points"))} under \armC. Holding the compared candidates fixed with respondent and candidate fixed effects, the regression-adjusted changes were \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Information acquisition", "Time - Baseline", " points"))} and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Information acquisition", "Cost - Baseline", " points"))}.

For selection, the baseline gap was $\Gamma_Y(B)=$ \Sexpr{live(effect_text(gamma_row("Registered selection", "Baseline"), 100, 1, " percentage points"))}, and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Registered selection", "Baseline", " points"))} after adjustment. Relative to that gap, the women-minus-men difference in selection changed by $\Psi_Y(T)=$ \Sexpr{live(effect_text(psi_row("Registered selection", "Time"), 100, 1, " points"))} under \armT\ and $\Psi_Y(C)=$ \Sexpr{live(effect_text(psi_row("Registered selection", "Cost"), 100, 1, " points"))} under \armC, with regression-adjusted estimates of \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Registered selection", "Time - Baseline", " points"))} and \Sexpr{live(adjusted_text("Registered selection", "Cost - Baseline", " points"))}. After Holm correction, \Sexpr{live(agreement_text("Information acquisition"))}, and \Sexpr{live(agreement_text("Registered selection"))}. Within the constrained environments, the selection gap was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(gamma_row("Registered selection", "Time"), 100, 1, " points"))} under \armT\ and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(gamma_row("Registered selection", "Cost"), 100, 1, " points"))} under \armC. These contrasts compare naturally occurring profiles of women and men; they show how the constraints redistribute selection between the two groups of real candidates, not the effect of changing a candidate's gender.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-gender, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=4.6, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Information acquisition and selection by candidate gender and decision environment}
\label{fig:gender}
\begin{notes}
Panel A: observed candidate-level means $\mu^R_g(z)$ for opening a candidate's description ($R=M$) and selecting the candidate ($R=Y$); filled circles are profiles of men, hollow circles profiles of women. Panel B: the tested estimands in percentage points, the baseline women-minus-men gap $\Gamma_R(B)$ and its change under each constraint, $\Psi_R(z)=\Gamma_R(z)-\Gamma_R(B)$. Filled circles are the main preregistered estimates from Equation~\eqref{eq:lpm}; hollow diamonds the complementary regression-adjusted estimates from Equations~\eqref{eq:psi} and~\eqref{eq:gamma} (respondent fixed effects, plus candidate fixed effects for $\Psi$). \Sexpr{format(paper_counts$candidate_appearances, big.mark = ",")} candidate appearances from \Sexpr{paper_counts$completed_respondents} eligible completed responses; 95\% intervals use two-way clustering by respondent and candidate. A retained task without a selection contributes zero selections for both profiles. Candidate gender is bundled with other characteristics of the real profiles.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Faces or facts?}
\label{sec:faces-facts}

Figure~\ref{fig:traits} asks which characteristics of the displayed profiles are associated with selection in each environment. The ideology of a candidate is a fact that respondents see only after opening a description, whereas gender, age, and facial dominance are visible in the photograph. The selection premium for ideologically congruent candidates---the difference in selection probability between profiles whose displayed ideology matched the respondent's self-placement and those that did not---was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(trait_row("Ideological congruence", "Baseline"), 100, 1, " percentage points", holm = FALSE))} under \armB, \Sexpr{live(effect_text(trait_row("Ideological congruence", "Time"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armT, and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(trait_row("Ideological congruence", "Cost"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armC. The corresponding association for high- versus low-dominance faces was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(trait_row("Dominance", "Baseline"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armB, \Sexpr{live(effect_text(trait_row("Dominance", "Time"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armT, and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(trait_row("Dominance", "Cost"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armC.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-candidate-traits, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=5.0, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Candidate characteristics, ideological congruence, and selection by decision environment}
\label{fig:traits}
\begin{notes}
Within-condition differences in the probability of a registered selection, in percentage points, with 95\% intervals clustered by respondent and candidate. The first four panels come from one linear probability model of \Sexpr{format(paper_counts$candidate_appearances, big.mark = ",")} candidate appearances with candidate gender, displayed ideology, dominance classification, and age group, their interactions with condition, randomized side, and task position; references are men, center, low dominance, and younger. The congruence panel comes from a separate model with congruence, its interaction with condition, side, and task position, among \Sexpr{live(congruence_n_respondents)} respondents with a numeric self-placement (left 1--4, center 5--6, right 7--10). Estimates are adjusted associations across real profile bundles, not causal effects of independently randomized attributes, and do not condition on opening information. Unadjusted, exploratory tests.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}

Respondents' own accounts offer a complementary view. Among tasks in which respondents would support their chosen candidate in a real election, the candidate's appearance or first impression was cited in \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Appearance or first impression", "Baseline"))} percent of \armB\ tasks, \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Appearance or first impression", "Time"))} percent of \armT\ tasks, and \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Appearance or first impression", "Cost"))} percent of \armC\ tasks (Figure~\ref{fig:reasons}). Where a description had been opened, the candidate's ideology was cited in \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Ideology shown", "Baseline"))}, \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Ideology shown", "Time"))}, and \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Ideology shown", "Cost"))} percent of tasks, and policy priorities in \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Policy priorities shown", "Baseline"))}, \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Policy priorities shown", "Time"))}, and \Sexpr{live(reason_share("Policy priorities shown", "Cost"))} percent, respectively. Stated reasons are post-choice self-reports and cannot by themselves establish which evidence drove a decision, but they show how respondents themselves weigh faces and facts.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-reasons, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=4.3, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Real-election support and stated reasons for the choice by decision environment}
\label{fig:reasons}
\begin{notes}
Shares of tasks with respondent-clustered 95\% intervals, truncated at 0 and 100. Real-election support is asked after every registered selection (\Sexpr{live(format(reason_counts$support_tasks, big.mark = ","))} tasks). Reasons are asked only after a positive answer (\Sexpr{live(format(reason_counts$reason_tasks, big.mark = ","))} tasks), and several may be chosen. Ideology and policy priorities are offered only when at least one description was opened, so their denominator is the \Sexpr{live(format(reason_counts$reason_revealed_tasks, big.mark = ","))} such tasks. Because each question follows post-treatment events, these shares are descriptive.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Dominant faces, candidate gender, and gender affinity}
\label{sec:dominance}

Figure~\ref{fig:dominance-gender} splits the dominance association by candidate gender, estimating Equation~\eqref{eq:dominance} separately in each condition. The dominance premium for women candidates minus that for men candidates ($\xi_z$) was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(dominance_gender_row("Baseline"), 100, 1, " percentage points", holm = FALSE))} under \armB, \Sexpr{live(effect_text(dominance_gender_row("Time"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armT, and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(dominance_gender_row("Cost"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armC. A positive value means that a dominant face raises the selection of women candidates more, or lowers it less, than that of men.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-dominance-gender, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=3.4, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Predicted selection by candidate gender and facial dominance in each decision environment}
\label{fig:dominance-gender}
\begin{notes}
Predicted probability that a displayed profile is selected, from Equation~\eqref{eq:dominance} estimated separately in each condition, averaging over the observed displayed ideology, age group, side, and task position; filled circles are profiles of men, hollow circles profiles of women. Each panel reports $\xi_z$, the women-minus-men difference in the dominance premium, with its 95\% interval and unadjusted $p$-value. Intervals use two-way clustering by respondent and candidate. The dominance classification does not apply one facial-width cut-off to both genders: median fWHR is \Sexpr{live(fwhr_median("Femenino", "Alta"))} for high- and \Sexpr{live(fwhr_median("Femenino", "Baja"))} for low-dominance women, and \Sexpr{live(fwhr_median("Masculino", "Alta"))} and \Sexpr{live(fwhr_median("Masculino", "Baja"))} for men, so $\xi_z$ compares dominance contrasts within each gender. Adjusted associations across real profiles, not causal effects of dominance or gender; exploratory.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:same-gender} recodes the respondent's and the candidate's gender as gender affinity. The dominance premium for candidates of the respondent's own gender minus that for candidates of the other gender ($\nu_z$, Equation~\eqref{eq:nu}) was \Sexpr{live(effect_text(same_gender_row("Baseline"), 100, 1, " percentage points", holm = FALSE))} under \armB, \Sexpr{live(effect_text(same_gender_row("Time"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armT, and \Sexpr{live(effect_text(same_gender_row("Cost"), 100, 1, " points", holm = FALSE))} under \armC. The joint test that respondent gender leaves the dominance-by-candidate-gender pattern unchanged in all three conditions gives \Sexpr{live(affinity_wald_text())}. Both sets of contrasts are exploratory and unadjusted for multiple comparisons. The affinity contrasts rest on \Sexpr{live(affinity_n_respondents)} respondents who identified as women or men, spread across six condition-by-gender groups.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-same-gender, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=3.4, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Predicted selection of same- and other-gender candidates by facial dominance}
\label{fig:same-gender}
\begin{notes}
Predicted probability that a displayed profile is selected, from Equation~\eqref{eq:affinity} estimated separately in each condition. Filled squares are candidates who share the respondent's gender (men rating men and women rating women, equally weighted); hollow squares are candidates of the other gender, weighted the same way, so the overall difference between women and men candidates cancels. Each panel reports $\nu_z$ (Equation~\eqref{eq:nu}) with its 95\% interval and unadjusted $p$-value; intervals use two-way clustering by respondent and candidate. \Sexpr{live(affinity_n_respondents)} respondents who identified as women or men; \Sexpr{live(affinity_excluded)} respondent(s) reporting another gender identity or none are outside this comparison only. Respondent gender is not randomized, so these are descriptive subgroup comparisons.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Profile-level choices}

Finally, Figure~\ref{fig:profiles} compares the selection probability of each real profile under each constraint with its probability under \armB, the estimands $\theta_c(z)$ and $\Delta_c(z)$. If the same candidates win regardless of how voters decide, points cluster around the diagonal; if constraints reshuffle the ranking, they scatter away from it. The current correlation between $\hat\theta_c(T)$ and $\hat\theta_c(B)$ is \Sexpr{live(profile_r("Time"))}, and between $\hat\theta_c(C)$ and $\hat\theta_c(B)$ it is \Sexpr{live(profile_r("Cost"))}. With a median of \Sexpr{live(median(profile_comparison$appearances))} appearances per profile and constrained condition, these correlations are attenuated by sampling noise and will become informative only as fieldwork accumulates.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
<<figure-profiles, fig.width=6.7, fig.height=3.7, eval=has_model_data>>=
@
\caption{Profile-specific selection probabilities under constraint and at baseline}
\label{fig:profiles}
\begin{notes}
Each point is one of the \Sexpr{paper_counts$eligible_profiles} real candidate profiles: its share of appearances that ended in its selection under \armT\ or \armC\ (vertical axis) against \armB\ (horizontal axis). The dashed line marks $\Delta_c(z)=0$. Point area is proportional to the profile's appearances in the constrained condition; hollow points are women. Side and rival are randomized, so these shares estimate $\theta_c(z)$ among completed responses. No profile-level estimate is interpreted individually.
\end{notes}
\end{figure}
\Sexpr{if (has_model_data) "" else "\\fi"}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The experiment examines whether randomized constraints alter political information acquisition, candidate choice, and the scrutiny applied to women candidates. Its central design choice is to treat appearance-based voting as the outcome of a search process: respondents always see the faces, and the decision environment governs how costly it is to go beyond them. This makes it possible to ask not only whether faces matter but when voters stop at them. The interim estimates in Section~\ref{sec:results} illustrate how the evidence will accumulate. They show how much programmatic information respondents request when it is free, how a binding deadline and an access cost change the timing of choice, and how the women-minus-men differences in search and selection move across environments. The congruence estimates indicate how strongly respondents use a candidate's ideology once they can see it, which determines how consequential the access constraints are for programmatic voting. Reading the preregistered differences together with the regression-adjusted models shows which of these patterns survive adjustment for chance imbalance in respondent characteristics. The interim sample is a fraction of the planned one, however, and the preregistered tests of the complete sample will determine which patterns are robust.

Several limits qualify the interpretation. First, the reported comparisons are restricted to eligible completed responses, so their causal interpretation depends on assumptions about completion-related selection; Appendix~\ref{app:collection} documents attrition by stage. Second, gender, ideology, party, and facial characteristics remain bundled within observed candidates. The crossed construction of the candidate pool makes the main classifications nearly independent, but gender-indexed comparisons still capture heterogeneity across naturally occurring profiles rather than isolated attribute effects. Third, opening a description shows that information was accessed, not that it was read, understood, or believed, and stated reasons are self-reports. Fourth, respondents are deliberately unfamiliar with Colombian politics. This removes recognition and partisan attachment, approximating voters who meet unfamiliar local candidates with little prior information, but it means that the magnitudes should not be read as estimates for Colombian voters. Within these limits, combining randomized decision environments, repeated choices, and behavioral measures of political learning connects appearance-based judgment to the acquisition and use of substantive information.

